</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></p>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 8</span>: </span><span id="S5.T8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison of AI-generated image attribution on the WildFake dataset under the cross-generator setting with different numbers of shots. The best score for each shot setting is highlighted in bold.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generator-Level Image Attribution:</span>
We further categorize the generative algorithms into three groups based on their model architectures: diffusion-based (DDPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, DDIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, ADM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, DALL·E <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, Stable Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, Midjourney <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, Imagen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, VQDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>), GAN-based (BigGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, StyleGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, GigaGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, StarGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, DF-GAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, GALIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>), and other (VQVAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>), to evaluate cross-generator performance.
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 5.4 Ablation Studies and Analyses ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Attribution as Retrieval: Model-Agnostic AI-Generated Image Attribution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, our method achieves the best performance across all evaluation settings.
More specifically, the proposed low-bit generative fingerprints outperform the features extracted by DIRE and ESSP by 25.4% and 36.3% in terms of mAP, respectively, indicating their stronger suitability for fake image attribution.
As the number of images in the registered database increases, Rank-1 accuracy improves while the task of ranking similarities across all images becomes progressively more challenging.</p>
</div>
